Modified for BenCode with MonoCode's backdrop blur (MonoCode's
`vendor/gpui-pre-apple`, branch `mc/migrate-monocode-to-gpui`):

- src/metal_renderer.rs: `backdrop_blur_marker(sigma, tint_alpha)` marks a
  quad as frosted glass; before drawing it, the renderer ends the pass,
  copies what is already drawn behind it, blurs it with
  `MPSImageGaussianBlur` and writes it back inside the quad's rounded bounds
  (`blur_backdrop`), then goes on in a new pass. The layer stays readable
  (`framebufferOnly` off) in every build so the drawable can be copied.
- src/shaders.metal: `backdrop_composite_vertex` / `_fragment`.

GPUI draws a quad with rounded corners as pieces (its interior, corners
squared off, and four strips, each the whole quad under its own mask), so
a frosted quad arrives in pieces too. Consecutive pieces of one quad
(`same_frost`) share one blur, and each writes only inside its mask but is
edged by the whole quad's rounded bounds, so the pieces meet without a
seam; a frosted card's shadow takes its radius from the strips.

GPUI draws opaque rectangles first, front to back, against a depth buffer.
What a frosted quad copies, and what a cutout clears, must not hold what is
drawn after it, so the opaque pass stops at the first such quad and
`quad_vertex` draws the opaque rectangles after it in order
(`bind_frame_inputs` passes the count in `QuadInputIndex_QuadCount`); a pass
that follows a blur starts its depth afresh.

And so the glass shows: a frosted card's own drop shadow is not drawn under
it, as CSS `box-shadow` is not, instead of filling it underneath (which the
blur spread dark across the card). GPUI gives a drop shadow no element
bounds, so `frosted_shadows` matches each to the first frosted quad drawn
after it within its reach and passes that quad's bounds in the shadow's
unused `corner_radii` (radius in `pad`), which `shadow_fragment` reads.

And so a quiet app gives GPU memory back (after MonoCode's `trim`,
BenCode's `src/gpu_trim.rs` calling it):

- src/metal_atlas.rs: every atlas is registered (on the main thread);
  `clear_all` drops their tiles and textures (gpui-pre has no
  `PlatformAtlas::clear` to do it).
- src/metal_renderer.rs: `trim()` clears the atlases and has each
  renderer's next frame reset the instance buffers to their first 2MB and
  drop its depth buffer; the backdrop's textures go once no frame has
  frosted anything for 2s.

And so streaming text shapes cheaper (MonoCode's f8540190):
src/text_system.rs keeps the sized CoreText fonts it shapes with
(`sized_fonts`, up to 512) instead of copying a font for every run.

And so a card fading by opacity fades its frost too (MonoCode da39885e eases
the blur by hand): the marker carries its tint's alpha, and the renderer
scales the blur by how far the element's opacity has faded that tint.

And so native views behind GPUI (the in-app browser WKWebView and HTML
artifacts) stay visible behind dialogs and never clip GPUI tooltips/popovers:
- src/metal_renderer.rs: `hole_punch_marker(pass_through)` clears a quad's
  clipped bounds to `rgba(0, 0, 0, 0)` using a non-blending quad pipeline
  (`clear_quads_pipeline_state`), keeps `CAMetalLayer.opaque` off while a
  cutout is present (as GPUI does for its own native views), and supplies a
  dark frosted backing in `backdrop_composite_fragment` where frosted popups
  overlap a cutout.

Chat backgrounds use `chat_background_corners` to mark a sprite. The image
shader applies the pane-relative fade and Haze after GPUI cover-crops the art,
in the same draw as the image (`chat_background_fragment`, its pipeline built
by the first frame that draws one).

Everything else is the crates.io source unchanged. To move to a new
gpui-pre, copy its `gpui-pre-apple` here and carry those changes over.
